    "label": "thm:regret-finite",
    "number": "Theorem 1",
    "quote": "Suppose, for all $t$, $\\ett$ is $1$-sub-Gaussian, $\\Ct \\subseteq [-1,1]^d$, and $||\\xt||_2 \\leq 1$ for all $\\xt\\in\\Ct$, and $||\\b|| \\leq 1$. Then, \\rflb, \\rfmlb, and \\rfub are fair algorithms ... With probability $1-\\delta$, for $j \\in \\{1, m, k\\}$, the regret of \\rfj is $R(T) = O\\left(d k j \\sqrt{T}\\log\\left(\\frac{T}{\\delta}\\right)\\right)$"
